CTI — Common Test Infrastructure
===============================

CTI runs compiler regression tests and application tests, records their
results, and generates scripts for reproducing failures. It consists of
Perl and shell drivers, an optional Java Distributed Test Manager (dTM),
and an optional CGI/PHP web interface. This is the historical Open64 CTI
source distribution; modern platform compatibility needs validation.

Start with doc/guide/index.md, or open doc/guide/index.html in a browser.
The documentation is reconstructed from the code at commit 8c3d603.

  Installation and platform requirements: doc/guide/installation.md
  Local runs, commands, and failure triage: doc/guide/running-tests.md
  Options and hierarchical customization: doc/guide/configuration.md
  Adding regression and application tests: doc/guide/adding-tests.md
  Distributed testing and schedules:       doc/guide/distributed.md
  Architecture and maintenance:            doc/guide/development.md
  Generated options and result catalogs:   doc/guide/reference.md

Repository: https://github.com/open64-compiler/opencti

There is no top-level package build. Configure the scripts using
"perl configure.pl" on your deployment host after reviewing the installation
guide. Configuration edits files in place; it also checks Java even for
sites that plan to run tests locally. Compilers are supplied separately.

Rebuild the documentation with Python 3 (standard library only):

    python3 doc/tools/build_docs.py
    python3 doc/tools/build_docs.py --check

The build refreshes the reference catalog from conf/*.conf and renders the
Markdown guides as static HTML. No CTI server or web server is needed to
read the guides. The existing HTML and text documentation entry points are rebuilt from
these guides, with implementation behavior taking precedence over old prose.

On case-insensitive filesystems, some OpenMP C/C++ test filenames collide.
Use a case-sensitive filesystem for a complete test checkout. The Git object
database retains the original files, even when the working tree cannot.
